\title{Infinitely Many Sign Changes of Symmetric Power \texorpdfstring{$L$}{L}-Function Coefficients over Sums of \texorpdfstring{$m$}{m} Squares, for All Even \texorpdfstring{$m$}{m}}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-01}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $f$ be a normalized Hecke eigenform of even weight $k \ge 2$ for the full modular group, let $j \ge 1$, and let $\lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n)$ denote the real Dirichlet coefficients of the $j$-th symmetric power $L$-function attached to $f$. It is known that these coefficients exhibit infinitely many sign changes as $n$ ranges over sums of $m$ squares, but only for the finite range $2 \le m \le 12$. We identify the elementary structural core responsible for the phenomenon and remove the upper restriction entirely: the sign-change conclusion holds for all even $m \ge 2$ (indeed for all integers $m \ge 2$). The argument rests on two facts about the representation sets $S_m = \{\, n : n \text{ is a sum of } m \text{ squares} \,\}$: they are \emph{nested}, $S_2 \subseteq S_3 \subseteq \cdots$, via padding representations with zeros; and they \emph{saturate}, $S_m = \N$ for every $m \ge 4$, by Lagrange's four-square theorem. Consequently the whole family collapses to the single base case $m = 2$, and for $m \ge 4$ the restricted problem is literally the unrestricted one. We also isolate a self-contained oscillation principle: if the partial sums of a real sequence are unbounded above and below, the sequence changes sign infinitely often.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} symmetric power $L$-functions, Hecke eigenforms, sign changes, sums of squares, Lagrange four-square theorem, partial sums, oscillation.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{MSC:} 11F30, 11F66, 11E25, 11N37.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Sign changes of arithmetic coefficients}
A pervasive question in the analytic theory of automorphic forms concerns the \emph{signs} of the coefficients of the associated $L$-functions. Given a real arithmetic function $n \mapsto a(n)$, one asks whether it changes sign infinitely often. For coefficients coming from modular forms and their symmetric powers, sign changes reflect deep equidistribution and cancellation phenomena.

Let $f$ be a normalized Hecke eigenform of even weight $k \ge 2$ for $\mathrm{SL}(2,\Z)$. For each $j \ge 1$ the $j$-th symmetric power lift produces an $L$-function whose Dirichlet coefficients we denote $\lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n) \in \R$. It is classical that the full sequence $\big(\lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n)\big)_{n \ge 1}$ changes sign infinitely often.

\subsection{Restricting to sums of squares}
Writing the positive and negative sub-samples as
\[
\mathcal{P}_m = \{\, n : n \in \Sm,\ \lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n) > 0 \,\}, \qquad
\mathcal{N}_m = \{\, n : n \in \Sm,\ \lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n) < 0 \,\},
\]
the question is whether both are infinite. Prior work established this for $2 \le m \le 12$. The restriction was a byproduct of the analytic methods, not intrinsic to the problem.

\begin{theorem}[Main Theorem, informal]
For every normalized Hecke eigenform $f$ of even weight $k \ge 2$, every $j \ge 1$, and every even integer $m \ge 2$, the coefficients $\lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n)$ change sign infinitely often as $n$ ranges over sums of $m$ squares; i.e. $\mathcal{P}_m$ and $\mathcal{N}_m$ are both infinite. The same holds for all integers $m \ge 2$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Strategy}
We disentangle the arithmetic geometry of the sampling sets from the analytic input about the coefficients. Two structural facts about $S_m = \{\, n \in \N : n \text{ is a sum of } m \text{ squares} \,\}$ do all the work: \emph{nesting} $S_2 \subseteq S_3 \subseteq \cdots$ (padding with zeros), and \emph{saturation} $S_m = \N$ for $m \ge 4$ (Lagrange plus nesting). Nesting propagates sign changes upward; saturation identifies the $m \ge 4$ case with the unrestricted one. The window $2 \le m \le 12$ reflected only the reach of the case-by-case method, and the ``even $m$'' phrasing is inessential.

\section{The representation sets and their structure}
Throughout, $\N = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$ and squares are of natural numbers; this loses no generality, since an integer square is a natural square.

\subsection{Definitions}
\begin{definition}[Sum of $m$ squares]
For $m, n \in \N$, we say $n$ \emph{is a sum of $m$ squares}, written $\IsSum(m,n)$, if there is $v \colon \{0,\dots,m-1\} \to \N$ with $\sum_{i=0}^{m-1} v(i)^2 = n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Representation set]
For $m \in \N$ define $\Sm = \{\, n \in \N : \IsSum(m,n) \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Nesting via padding}
\begin{lemma}[Append a zero]\label{lem:succ}
If $\IsSum(m,n)$ then $\IsSum(m+1,n)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Extend a length-$m$ representation $v$ by a final coordinate $0$; it contributes $0^2 = 0$, leaving the sum unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity of representability]\label{lem:mono}
If $j \le m$ and $\IsSum(j,n)$, then $\IsSum(m,n)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Given $v$ of length $j$, set $w(i) = v(i)$ for $i < j$ and $w(i) = 0$ for $j \le i < m$. Splitting $\sum_{i<m} w(i)^2$ at $j$ gives $\sum_{i<j} v(i)^2 = n$ plus a block of zeros.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nesting]\label{cor:nest}
If $j \le m$ then $S_j \subseteq \Sm$; in particular $S_2 \subseteq S_3 \subseteq S_4 \subseteq \cdots$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Squares and infinitude}
\begin{lemma}[A single square]\label{lem:onesq}
For every $k \in \N$, $\IsSum(1, k^2)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Every square is a sum of $m$ squares]\label{lem:sq}
If $m \ge 1$, then $k^2 \in \Sm$ for every $k \in \N$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Combine Lemma~\ref{lem:onesq} with Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} using $1 \le m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Infinitude]\label{prop:inf}
For every $m \ge 1$, the set $\Sm$ is infinite.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The map $k \mapsto k^2$ is injective on $\N$ and, by Lemma~\ref{lem:sq}, lands in $\Sm$; an injective image of an infinite set is infinite.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Saturation from the four-square theorem}
\begin{theorem}[Lagrange, 1770]\label{thm:lagrange}
Every $n \in \N$ satisfies $n = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2$ for some $a,b,c,d \in \N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Four squares suffice]\label{cor:four}
For every $n \in \N$, $\IsSum(4,n)$; i.e. $S_4 = \N$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation]\label{thm:sat}
For every $m \ge 4$, $S_m = \N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:four}, $n \in S_4$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} with $4 \le m$, $n \in \Sm$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The sparse regimes]
The saturation threshold is exactly $m=4$. For $m=3$, Legendre's theorem shows $S_3$ omits precisely the integers $4^a(8b+7)$; and $S_2$ is genuinely sparse (its counting function up to $X$ is $\asymp X/\sqrt{\log X}$, by Landau). Thus only $m = 2, 3$ are nontrivial.
\end{remark}

\section{Sign changes and the reduction}
We abstract the coefficient sequence to an arbitrary real sequence and reintroduce the automorphic input only as a hypothesis about the base case.

\begin{definition}[Infinitely many sign changes over a set]
Let $a \colon \N \to \R$ and $T \subseteq \N$. We say $a$ \emph{has infinitely many sign changes over $T$}, written $\HasSign(a,T)$, if both $\{\, n \in T : a(n) > 0 \,\}$ and $\{\, n \in T : a(n) < 0 \,\}$ are infinite.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Sign changes propagate upward]\label{lem:signmono}
Let $a \colon \N \to \R$ and $T \subseteq U \subseteq \N$. If $\HasSign(a,T)$, then $\HasSign(a,U)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The positive (resp.\ negative) sub-sample over $T$ is contained in that over $U$; a superset of an infinite set is infinite.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reduction to $m=2$]\label{thm:reduction}
Let $a \colon \N \to \R$. If $\HasSign(a, S_2)$, then $\HasSign(a, \Sm)$ for every integer $m \ge 2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $m \ge 2$, $S_2 \subseteq \Sm$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:nest}; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:signmono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main Theorem]\label{thm:main}
Let $f$ be a normalized Hecke eigenform of even weight $k \ge 2$ for $\mathrm{SL}(2,\Z)$, let $j \ge 1$, and set $a = \lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}$. If $\HasSign(a, S_2)$, then for every even integer $m \ge 2$, $\HasSign(a, \Sm)$: the coefficients $\lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n)$ are positive for infinitely many sums of $m$ squares and negative for infinitely many sums of $m$ squares.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:reduction}, which yields the conclusion for all $m \ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Collapse for $m \ge 4$]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $a \colon \N \to \R$ and $m \ge 4$. Then $\HasSign(a, \Sm) \iff \HasSign(a, \N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sat}, $\Sm = \N$, so the predicates coincide.
\end{proof}

\section{An oscillation engine from unbounded partial sums}

\begin{definition}[Partial sums]
For $a \colon \N \to \R$ and $X \in \N$, set $P(X) = \sum_{n < X} a(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided unbounded partial sums force oscillation]\label{thm:osc}
Let $a \colon \N \to \R$. If $P(X)$ is unbounded above and unbounded below, then $a$ is positive infinitely often and negative infinitely often; i.e. $\HasSign(a,\N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a(n) \le 0$ for all $n \ge N_0$, then $P$ is non-increasing beyond $N_0$, hence bounded above by $\max\{P(0),\dots,P(N_0)\}$, contradicting unboundedness above. So $a(n) > 0$ infinitely often. Symmetrically, if $a(n) \ge 0$ for all $n \ge N_1$, then $P$ is eventually non-decreasing and bounded below, contradicting unboundedness below; so $a(n) < 0$ infinitely often.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:osc} isolates exactly the analytic input needed --- two-sided growth of a summatory function --- and requires no cancellation estimates beyond it. For the sequences at hand, Rankin--Selberg-type techniques furnish such two-sided growth, converting growth statements directly into the base-case hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion and future work}
The lesson is that the shape of the constraint ``sum of $m$ squares'' is nearly irrelevant: it dissolves for $m \ge 4$ and, via nesting, never obstructs the transfer from the base case.

\paragraph{Uniform density independent of $m$.} For fixed $f, j$, the count of sign changes among sums of $m$ squares up to $X$ should grow at the same order of magnitude for every $m \ge 4$, dominating the $m=2$ count; all $m$-dependence lives in $m = 2, 3$.

\paragraph{The two-square case as universal bottleneck.} Infinitely many sign changes over $\Sm$ (any $m \ge 2$) should be equivalent to their existence over $S_2$. Nesting gives sufficiency; the two-square case, where Gaussian-integer structure is richest, is the natural focus.

\paragraph{From unbounded partial sums to guaranteed oscillation.} For these sequences restricted to $S_2$, the partial sums should be unbounded above and below, forcing sign changes by Theorem~\ref{thm:osc}.

\paragraph{Sparse-set thresholds beyond squares.} One may seek analogous saturation and nesting for other additive representation families (higher powers, mixed forms), and the thresholds separating sparse from unrestricted sampling.

\section{Conclusion}
Separating the arithmetic geometry of sums of squares from the analytic facts about symmetric power coefficients removes the artificial bound in the sign-change theorem. Nesting transfers the base case upward and saturation identifies the restricted problem with the unrestricted one, so $\lambda_{\mathrm{sym}^j f}(n)$ changes sign infinitely often over sums of $m$ squares for all even $m \ge 2$ --- indeed for all $m \ge 2$ --- with all content concentrated in the base case $m = 2$, accessible through the partial-sum oscillation criterion.

